\details{
Every class has an \code{expr}, a function whose formals are the state args and
whose body checks them, as a constructor checks its args, and returns what
interpolation makes the block expression from (see \code{\link[=interpolate]{interpolate()}}): a call,
a string, a function of the data inputs or an object of a class that
inherits from \code{blockr_expr}, such as what \code{\link[=bbquote]{bbquote()}} returns. An arg
without a default starts unset and stays out of the block's state until
something sets it. A body that uses an unset arg, or signals \code{invalid()}
with a message, makes R run nothing and report the block as \code{invalid}, and
a body that can do without an unset arg tests it with \code{\link[=missing]{missing()}}. Called
without args, \code{expr} gives the state a new block starts with.

The data inputs, apart from the state args, are given as \code{inputs}: a
character vector names them, such as \code{"data"}, \code{c("x", "y")} or \code{"..."} for
a variadic block, a list from \code{\link[=alist]{alist()}} gives them as formals, and a
function gives them as its formals and checks them in its body, as
\code{function(data) stopifnot(is.data.frame(data))} does. No input has a
default. A class names its UI module with \code{js_module()}, which keeps the
module's path in the package's \verb{inst/} and the package whose code calls it,
for R to resolve when it serves the module, and which resolves the path
against the working directory outside a package. A class further takes a
named list of \code{requests}, for what its UI asks of R, each a function or made
by \code{\link[=new_request]{new_request()}}, which names the board options it reads (see
\code{\link[=block_request]{block_request()}}), and the coercion that makes an output from a result, as
\code{output}. A field a class leaves out is \code{NULL}.

A class is checked once, when it is defined: no state arg is \code{...},
\code{block_name} or \code{visible}, no input or state arg shares the name of
another, the arguments of each request name inputs or state args, but for
parameters, which have a default, for the \code{file} of an action and for those
that take board options, which are neither, and \code{expr}, called without
args, cannot run yet or returns a call, a string, a function whose formals
are the inputs or an object of a class that inherits from \code{blockr_expr},
holding no function.

The constructor has the formals of \code{expr}, a \code{block_name} and a \code{visible},
and no \code{...}, so a misspelled state arg fails as an unused argument. It
calls \code{expr} with its args and returns a block, a list of the state that
the checks in \code{expr} left in the frame it ran in, which leaves out an arg
that is still unset. A block holds its name, which defaults to one made
from its class's name by \code{default_block_name()}, and core's attribute
\code{visible}, which says which of the block's sections show, and refers to its
class, which every block of the class shares.
}
\section{Block vectors}{

Multiple blocks can be combined into a \code{blocks} object, a container for
an (ordered) set of blocks. Block IDs are handled at the \code{blocks} level,
which ensures that those given are unique and, as every ID, strings that
are neither empty nor made of digits alone. A block passed without a name
has no ID, which the client mints when it opens the board.
}

